src/framework/contracts/README.md:139:`verify` seals the body context immediately, snapshots outputs/correspondences before waiting, and allows only one verification in flight. Output analysis/facts and resource writes remain provisional until synchronous `commit()`. `rollback(reason)` discards them; `fail(error)` additionally retains the original error classification. A closed accepted execution cannot be rewritten as failed. `tx.settle()` drains started semantic work before an asynchronous host failure hook finalizes the error.
src/framework/contracts/README.md:153:`ResourcePort` exposes synchronous snapshot reads and an atomic synchronous commit of staged JSON writes against expected original revisions. `MemoryResources` is the included reference adapter. The port contract requires all-or-nothing commit: it validates all names/values and expected revisions before applying any write. Async external systems can be adapted by preparing a stable snapshot and using an appropriately transactional host commit boundary; they must not claim atomicity they cannot provide. Native async adapter methods and thenable commit results are rejected; rejected asynchronous adapter work is observed but cannot be undone by the manager. The host adapter remains responsible for its atomicity guarantee.
src/framework/contracts/README.md:157:Writes are staged and visible to subsequent reads in the same transaction. Preconditions can read the original resource state, while postconditions see staged revisions. Commit checks the physical original revisions for conflicts and validates post-write dependencies against the proposed final view before installing writes and facts. A rejected postcondition publishes neither the proposed resource state nor its facts.

